reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'New properties and functions will enable you to animate properties that, until now, were not animatable.We’ll look at the following properties:transition-behaviorcalc-size()@starting-styleused to:Change the height of an element from 0 to autoChange the value of display from none to blockThe codeThese are the properties we’ll use for these examples.transition-behaviorThe transition-behavior property specifies whether transitions will be started for properties whose animation behavior is discrete.!!! note Discrete definition\n' +
    'Discrete properties’ values are not additive, and interpolation swaps from the start value to the end value at 50%.Specifically, denoting p as the progress value:If p < 0.5, then V_result = V_startIf p ≥ 0.5, then V_result = V_end\n' +
    '!!!calc-size()The calc-size() function allows math to be performed on intrinsic sizes, just like you can use calc() to perform math on extrinsic sizes.@starting-styleThe @starting-style at-rule is used to define starting values for properties set on an element that you want to transition from when an element is first displayed on a previously loaded page.The @starting-style at-rule can be used in two ways:As a standalone block that contains one or more rulesets defining starting style declarations and selecting the elements they apply toNested within an existing ruleset, that contains one or more declarations defining starting property values for the elements already selected by that rulesetHow it worksThe HTML we’ll work with is simple.A button element with the class toggleA div element with the class container<button class="toggle">Toogle Content</button>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<div class="container">\n' +
    '  <h2 class="container__headline">Some Title</h2>\n' +
    '  <p>Some content</p>\n' +
    '</div>The container (.container) element holds the content of the dialogue.the allow-discrete rule enables transitions for properties like display & visibility. Their values are applied after the transition has finished.We also include the final values for the attributes we want to animate in case the browser doesn’t support the @starting-style at-rule..container {\n' +
    '  block-size: 0;\n' +
    '  padding-block: 0;\n' +
    '  display: none;\n' +
    '  transition: all 0.5s ease-in-out;\n' +
    '\ttransition-behavior: allow-discrete;\n' +
    '}We have separate CSS rules that match the container selector (.container) with the open attribute.The values for block-size, display and padding-block are the final values for the transition.calc-size will let us use intrinsic values in the calculations.The @starting-style rule tells the browser what properties to use as the initial state for the animation.If we don’t use it the transition may not happen or may work in unexpected ways..container[open] {\n' +
    '  block-size: auto;\n' +
    '  block-size: calc-size(auto);\n' +
    '  display: block;\n' +
    '  padding-block: 2rem;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  @starting-style {\n' +
    '    block-size: 0;\n' +
    '    padding-block: 0;\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '}there is a little Javascript in the project. We’ve added a pointerdown event to toggle the open attribute on the container element.const toggle = document.querySelector(".toggle");\n' +
    'const content = document.querySelector(".container");\n' +
    '\n' +
    'toggle.addEventListener("pointerdown", (e) => {\n' +
    '  content.toggleAttribute("open");\n' +
    '});Browser supportWe’re mostly waiting for Firefox to fully support the feature in Desktop browsers and to implement it in Firefox for Android.The support table for desktop browsers looks nice.ChromeEdgeSafariFirefox128 - 130<br>Supported127<br> Supported17.6 - TP<br>Supported129 - 131<br>Partial support<br><br>Doesn’t animate from display: noneThe mobile support is a little more spotty.Unknown support for Opera Mini and UC browsers likely means it’s not supported. As for other browsers, Firefox doesn’t provide support in its Android version, I wonder if it’s because a different team develops the Android versionChrome for AndroidSafari on iOS*Samsung InternetOpera Mini*Opera Mobile*UC Browser for AndroidAndroid Browser*Firefox for Android127<br>Supported17.5<br>Supported25<br>SupportedUnknown support80<br>Supported15.5<br>Unknown support127<br>Supported127<br>Not supportedWith this data in mind, we should look at these transitions as progressive enhancements at least until Firefox adds the feature in Android and finishes the feature for desktop.Further workWe’ve covered animating from display: none and to height: auto.A future step is to research what other discrete properties can we transition as described in Not Fully Animatable CSS properties.There are other properties similar to calc-size that would work with different types of discrete properties. These are:color-mix() for color interpolationcross-fade() for image interpolationtransform-mix() for interpolated transform valuesmix() for general-purpose interpolated valueIt’ll be interesting to see how these properties work once they are implemented.Video Tutorials<lite-youtube videoid="1VsMKz4Zweg"></lite-youtube><lite-youtube videoid="4prVdA7_6u0"></lite-youtube>Links and resourcestransition-behavior as specifiedtransition-behavior — MDNcalc-size as specified@starting-style — MDN',
  readingTime: { text: '4 min read', minutes: 3.165, time: 189900, words: 633 }
}
