reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'I was working on typesetting poems when I thought what it would take to center a piece of content both vertically and horizontally?For image and smaller blocks of text like poems, I choose to work with flexbox as it provides, in my opinion, the simplest way to center content on both axes without scripting.The code looks like this:.container {\n' +
    '  height: 100vh;\n' +
    '  display: flex;\n' +
    '  flex-flow: column;\n' +
    '  justify-content: center;\n' +
    '  align-items: center;\n' +
    '}Some caveats:You must set an explicit height for the container, in this case, 100vh. Otherwise, there is no height to center toBecause we’re centering all the content it may be a good idea to create an inner container or paragraphs and other individual elements may be centered and cause unexpected results.You can see working examples of this in my poema 6 and poema 19 demos. An editable example of Poema 6 is in Codepen.',
  readingTime: { text: '1 min read', minutes: 0.715, time: 42900, words: 143 }
}
remark plugin assigned minutesRead: 1 min read
reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'Centering content, particularly vertical centering, has always been very challenging to me.The old wayThese are some techniques to center content using CSS before Flexbox.Aligning textAligning text is easy. We use something like this to center align all paragraphs:p {\n' +
    '  text-align: center;\n' +
    '}or a class to center specified content:.center {\n' +
    '  text-align: center;\n' +
    '}Centering ContentMy favorite trick to center larger blocks of content is to use margin attributes with the auto value.If you use the two-value margin declaration then you need to remember that the first value is for the top and bottom margins, and the second value is for the right and left margins./* These are equivalent */\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.center {\n' +
    '  margin-left: auto;\n' +
    '  margin-right: auto;\n' +
    '  width: 100px\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.center {\n' +
    '  margin-inline: auto;\n' +
    '  width: 100px;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.center {\n' +
    '  margin: 0 auto;\n' +
    '  width: 100px;\n' +
    '}Vertical Centering Text or ObjectsVertical centering is much more complex and would require more code to accomplish the same effect.For this content:<div class="container">\n' +
    '  <div class="center">\n' +
    '    <p>This is an example</p>\n' +
    '  </div>\n' +
    '</div>Use the following CSS to center the “ element vertically on the screen..container {\n' +
    '  position: relative;\n' +
    '  text-align: center;\n' +
    '  display: block;\n' +
    '  height: 100%;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  &::before,\n' +
    '  .center {\n' +
    '    display: inline-block;\n' +
    '    vertical-align: middle;\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  &::before {\n' +
    '    content: "";\n' +
    '    width: 0;\n' +
    '    height: 100%;\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '}Using FlexboxFlexbox has simplified things considerably.For the Flexbox examples we will use the following HTML code.<div class="flexer-container">\n' +
    '  class="item one"></div>\n' +
    '  class="item two"></div>\n' +
    '  class="item three"></div>\n' +
    '</div>horizontal centering with Flexbox assumes the followingWe’re working with rowsWe don’t have enough items for the layout to wrapWe didn’t set up the layout to wrapThe individual items (elements with class .item) need to have dimensions or they won’t lay out properly..flexer-container {\n' +
    '  display: flex;\n' +
    '  flex-direction: row;\n' +
    '  justify-content: center;\n' +
    '  gap: 0px;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.item {\n' +
    '  align-self: auto;\n' +
    '  height: 150px;\n' +
    '  width: 150px;\n' +
    '  border: 4px solid oklch(0 0 0);\n' +
    '}Centering content vertically uses almost identical code than the one we use for inline centering.The difference is that, instead of using justify-content: center; we use align-items: center; to handle vertical centering.The container element must have a height descriptor for vertical alignment to work..flexer-container {\n' +
    '  display: flex;\n' +
    '  align-items: center;\n' +
    '  gap: 10px;\n' +
    '  height: 800px\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.item {\n' +
    '  height: 150px;\n' +
    '  width: 150px;\n' +
    '  border: 2px solid oklch(0 0 0);\n' +
    '}The final centering technique is to both center vertically and horizontally.Using both justify-content and align-items to place the content at the center of the screen both vertically and horizontally..flexer-container {\n' +
    '  display: flex;\n' +
    '  justify-content: center;\n' +
    '  align-items: center;\n' +
    '  gap: 10px;\n' +
    '  height: 800px;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '.item {\n' +
    '  height: 150px;\n' +
    '  width: 150px;\n' +
    '  border: 2px solid oklch(0 0 0);\n' +
    '}',
  readingTime: { text: '3 min read', minutes: 2.035, time: 122100, words: 407 }
}
remark plugin assigned minutesRead: 3 min read
reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'One of the most frustrating things for me is how to center things using CSS.There are many ways to do it and everyone disagrees with everyone else. It is also true that there is no one-size-fits-all solution because what works with modern browsers doesn’t necessarily work in older versions.That said, my favorite way to center content is using Flexbox.For example, the code below will center its children both vertically and horizontally on the available screen size (100vw by 100vh).Because we’re using a right-to-left and top-to-bottom language we’ve set the direction to a column, otherwise second and subsequent elements would appear next to each other.We can choose whether to align vertically or horizontally and we can also choose the size of the area where we want to center content if using it as a standalone element..container {\n' +
    '  width: 100vw;\n' +
    '  height: 100vh;\n' +
    '  display: flex;\n' +
    '  flex-direction: column;\n' +
    '  /* Vertical alignment*/\n' +
    '  align-items: center;\n' +
    '  /* Horizontal alignment */\n' +
    '  justify-content: center;\n' +
    '}Using this code as a starting point we can experiment with what it would take to center content that is nested or inside grid cells.',
  readingTime: { text: '1 min read', minutes: 0.9, time: 54000, words: 180 }
}
remark plugin assigned minutesRead: 1 min read
reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'There are times when I need to check out a specific tag from a repository. I do this often enough that I chose to document the process.A concrete example: I need to get the latest revision of the Gutenberg plugin so I can compile it and install it on my testing machine.Because there is no branch, I can’t just do: git checkout -b <branch-name>So I had to research how to get creative in doing this.The first step is to make sure we have all the tags available both to our origin and any upstream repositories.We do this with git’s fetch command and two flags:—all To fetch from all configured remotes—tagsFetch all remote tags into local tags with the same name, in addition to whatever else would otherwise be fetchedgit fetch --all --tagsWe want to work with v13.3.0 so we need to get the tag:git fetch --all --tags\n' +
    'Fetching origin\n' +
    'Fetching upstream\n' +
    'remote: Enumerating objects: 502, done.\n' +
    'remote: Counting objects: 100% (459/459), done.\n' +
    'remote: Compressing objects: 100% (240/240), done.\n' +
    'remote: Total 502 (delta 283), reused 327 (delta 218), pack-reused 43\n' +
    'Receiving objects: 100% (502/502), 1.29 MiB | 7.24 MiB/s, done.\n' +
    'Resolving deltas: 100% (289/289), completed with 102 local objects.\n' +
    'From https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg\n' +
    '   046ca65b20..81ac4cf385  trunk      -> upstream/trunk\n' +
    ' * [new tag]               v13.3.0    -> v13.3.0Once you know the name of the tag, you can run the following command to check it out:git checkout tags/v13.3.0 -b v13.0.0-branchtags/v13.3.0 is the name of the tag we want to work with.The -b flag tells git to create a branch named v13.0.0-branch and check it out.',
  readingTime: { text: '2 min read', minutes: 1.28, time: 76800, words: 256 }
}
remark plugin assigned minutesRead: 2 min read
reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'When working with web content, it is important to check what browsers your content is compatible with.There are three ways to do this:Web UIcaniuse provides a basic compatibility table for most major desktop and mobile browsers.MDN goes a step further than Caniuse, it also provides additional information about the property, how it works and the specification that the property belongs to.Both Caniuse and MDN require you to have a browser open on the right site and actively search for the property or API you’re looking for.Programmatic ToolsMDN provides its browser compatibility data as an NPM package and Can I use provides node-caniuseYou can use either of these packages as  the database for your own projects.Command Line ToolsSometimes you just want to make sure that something will work on the browsers you are targetting but don’t want to open a new browser tab to check it.caniuse-cmd gives you one tool to do this.You can choose two methods of using the caniuse CLI.You can install it globally using the following command:npm i -g caniuse-cmdYou can also choose to install it as a development dependency. This will pin the version of caniuse to a specific point in time.npm i -D caniuse-cmdOnce it’s installed you can run it using a command like this:caniuse nestingand you’ll get a result like this (broken into multiple lines for readability):CSS Nesting ✔ 2.16% ◒ 78.26%\n' +
    '  IE ✘  Edge ✘ 12+ ✘ 109+¹ ◒ 112+³ Firefox ✘ 2+ ✘ 115+² ✔ 117+\n' +
    '  Chrome ✘ 4+ ✘ 109+¹ ◒ 112+³ Safari ✘ 3.1+ ◒ 16.5+³\n' +
    '  Opera ✘ 9+ ✘ 95+¹ ◒ 98+³While this is not a perfect solution, it doesn’t help with syntax, it helps to keep your hands on the keyboard as you work and gives you the minimal necessary information about support for a technology on y our target browsers.',
  readingTime: { text: '2 min read', minutes: 1.51, time: 90600, words: 302 }
}
