reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'I don’t hear about this very often but once in a while, I will hear about building print stylesheets so that web pages will look exactly the same in print as they do on screen.While I think the idea is laughable, it made me think about what it would take to make web content printable. This is different than using all of CSS Paged Media (as documented in Creating printable content from the web), we might test part of the Paged Media and Generated Content specifications but I’m not confident that they’ll work in all browsers.What we want to do:Put consistent margins around the text so it doesn’t print right on the border of the pageWhere possible make the text 80 characters wide and adjust the line-height of the text accordinglyChange the background color to white and the text color to blackAdd the link text to all URLsRemove the image and replace it with the content of the figcaption element if it existsSomething to make clear from the beginning: the ideas in this post will not create a printed document that looks exactly the same as the page on the screen. Quite the opposite; this is meant to create something that is easier to read and doesn’t require a full laser printer toner cartridge to print a page because of the images in it.Getting startedHTML has had a way to link stylesheets for different media formats since the early days. The syntax is simple, something like:<link   href="print.css"\n' +
    '        rel="stylesheet"\n' +
    '        media="print">In the print stylesheet, we can add print-specific items that will enhance the printed page without changing the way the page looks on screens.To make sure that the styles in the print stylesheet don’t conflict with our regular styles, we need to wrap them in a @media print block. Print styles must be the last declaration on a page or they will be overridden by later declarations.@media print {\n' +
    '\n' +
    '}What can we doHere are some ideas of what we can do with CSS to create a better reading experience.Setting page marginsBy default printed web pages have no margins unless you assign them on the stylesheet. From Experience, reading things flush to the margin is hard. To account for this we set equal margins for all sides of the page.body {\n' +
    '  margin: 2em;\n' +
    '}If we expect users to bind the printed pages, then we can use either margin-left and define each margin individually, or the four-value version of margin./* These two declarations are equivalent */\n' +
    'body {\n' +
    '  margin-top: 1em;\n' +
    '  margin-right: 1em;\n' +
    '  margin-bottom: 1em;\n' +
    '  margin-left: 2em;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'body {\n' +
    '  /* Clockwise: top, right, bottom, left */\n' +
    '  margin: 1em 1em 1em 2em;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    'Adding the URL to linksThere is nothing more frustrating than seeing a link and knowing it is a link but not knowing what the URL is and not being able to copy/paste it to the browser. We can fix this by appending the value of the href attribute to the link it references.a[href^="http"]:after {\n' +
    '  content: " (" attr(href) ")";\n' +
    '}Hiding images and adding alt textWhile it would be nice to keep images in the printed output there are issues with excessive tuner/ink consumption that can make it so you can’t print the entire document.To handle that, we’ll remove images and captions inside figures, and insert the image’s alt attribute where the image would go.figure > img {\n' +
    '  display: none;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'figure > figcaption {\n' +
    '  display: none;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '/* We might not always want this */\n' +
    'figure > img:after {\n' +
    '  content: "Image: " attr(alt) ;\n' +
    '}Handling typographyTypography on screen is different than typography in print. I personally like to do the following:Replace the font with one of Roboto, Verdana, or HelveticaTweak the line-height so it’ll be comfortable to readSet the top, right, and bottom margins to one inch and the left margin to two inchesThis assumes left-hand binding. As far as I know, there’s no way to do alternate page binding with CSS that doesn’t include paged media extensions@font-face { /* 1 */\n' +
    '  font-family: "Roboto";\n' +
    '  src: url("/fonts/Roboto-Regular-webfont.woff2") format("woff2");\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'body {\n' +
    '  font-family: "Roboto", sans-serif; /* 1 */\n' +
    '  line-height: 1.25; /* 2 */\n' +
    '  margin: 1in 1in 1in 2in; /* 3 */\n' +
    '}',
