reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: "When working with publishing content in the future, the hardest thing is to keep track of when things are scheduled to go live. The Eleventy Publishing Calendar Plugin provides a visual calendar interface to help content creators and editors see their publishing schedule at a glance.This post provides an explainer of the Eleventy Publishing Calendar Plugin, breaking down the code into its key components and explaining the process step by step.OverviewThe plugin creates a graphical calendar view for Eleventy’s publishing schedule. It gathers post metadata, groups posts by date, and generates an HTML grid calendar with navigation controls. The calendar displays post titles as links, along with the source markdown file names, and supports customization through options.Code Structure1. Module Setup and DependenciesThe plugin starts by importing the Temporal API and defining default options.import { Temporal } from '@js-temporal/polyfill';\n" +
    '\n' +
    'export default function(eleventyConfig, options = {}) {\n' +
    '  const defaultOptions = {\n' +
    "    collections: ['posts'],\n" +
    "    calendarClass: 'publishing-calendar',\n" +
    "    dayClass: 'calendar-day',\n" +
    "    postClass: 'calendar-post',\n" +
    "    navClass: 'calendar-nav',\n" +
    '    colors: {\n' +
    "      header: '#f0f0f0',\n" +
    "      today: '#e0f7fa',\n" +
    "      past: '#f5f5f5',\n" +
    "      future: '#fff'\n" +
    '    },\n' +
    "    layout: 'grid'\n" +
    '  };\n' +
    '\n' +
    "  const opts = { ...defaultOptions, ...options };This section:Imports Temporal for date handling (uses polyfill for compatibility)Defines default configuration options for styling and behaviorMerges user-provided options with defaultsTemporal API NoteWhile the Temporal API is at stage 3 in the TC39 process (meaning it’s a candidate for inclusion in the ECMAScript standard), browser support is not yet ready for production use without a polyfill. This plugin uses the @js-temporal/polyfill package to ensure compatibility across different Node.js versions and future browser environments. As browser support improves, the plugin will prioritize native Temporal usage over the polyfill.2. Shortcode RegistrationThe plugin registers a shortcode with Eleventy that generates the calendar HTML.eleventyConfig.addShortcode('publishingCalendar', function(month, year) {\n" +
    '  // ... implementation\n' +
    '});The shortcode accepts optional month and year parameters to display a specific month.3. Date CalculationThe shortcode calculates the target month and determines the date range for the calendar grid.const currentDate = Temporal.Now.plainDateISO();\n' +
    'const targetMonth = month ? Temporal.PlainYearMonth.from({ year: parseInt(year), month: parseInt(month) }) : Temporal.PlainYearMonth.from(currentDate);\n' +
    'const startOfMonth = targetMonth.toPlainDate({ day: 1 });\n' +
    'const endOfMonth = targetMonth.toPlainDate({ day: targetMonth.daysInMonth });\n' +
    'const startOfWeek = startOfMonth.subtract({ days: startOfMonth.dayOfWeek - 1 }); // Monday start\n' +
    'const endOfWeek = endOfMonth.add({ days: 7 - endOfMonth.dayOfWeek });This code:Gets the current date using TemporalDetermines the target month (current or specified)Calculates the start and end dates of the monthExtends the range to include full weeks for a complete grid4. Post CollectionThe plugin collects posts from specified Eleventy collections.// Collect posts\n' +
    'const allPosts = [];\n' +
    'opts.collections.forEach(collectionName => {\n' +
    '  if (this.ctx.collections[collectionName]) {\n' +
    '    allPosts.push(...this.ctx.collections[collectionName]);\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '});This loops through the configured collections and gathers all posts into a single array.5. Post Grouping and SerializationPosts are grouped by their publication date and serialized for client-side use.// Group posts by date and simplify for client-side\n' +
    'const postsByDate = {};\n' +
    'allPosts.forEach(post => {\n' +
    '  if (post.date) {\n' +
    '    const postDate = Temporal.PlainDate.from(post.date);\n' +
    '    const dateKey = postDate.toString();\n' +
    '    if (!postsByDate[dateKey]) postsByDate[dateKey] = [];\n' +
    '    postsByDate[dateKey].push({\n' +
    '        title: post.data.title,\n' +
    '        url: post.url,\n' +
    "        fileName: post.inputPath.split('/').pop(),\n" +
    "        contentType: post.data?.contentType || 'post'\n" +
    '    });\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '});This creates an object where keys are ISO date strings and values are arrays of simplified post objects (title, URL, filename, content type) for that date. This data is later injected into the client-side script.6. HTML Generation - NavigationThe plugin generates the calendar HTML, starting with navigation controls.let html = `<div id="${calendarId}" class="${opts.calendarClass}">`;\n' +
    'html += `<div class="${opts.navClass}">`;\n' +
    'const prevMonth = targetMonth.subtract({ months: 1 });\n' +
    'const nextMonth = targetMonth.add({ months: 1 });\n' +
    'html += `<a href="?month=${prevMonth.month}&year=${prevMonth.year}" class="calendar-nav-prev">Previous</a>`;\n' +
    "html += `<h2>${targetMonth.toLocaleString('en', { month: 'long', year: 'numeric' })}</h2>`;\n" +
    'html += `<a href="?month=${nextMonth.month}&year=${nextMonth.year}" class="calendar-nav-next">Next</a>`;\n' +
    'html += `</div>`;This creates a navigation bar with Previous/Next links and the current month/year title. Note the unique id assigned to the container and the specific classes for navigation links, which are used by the client-side script.7. HTML Generation - Grid HeaderThe calendar grid starts with day-of-week headers.html += `<div class="calendar-grid">`;\n' +
    "const daysOfWeek = ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'];\n" +
    'daysOfWeek.forEach(day => {\n' +
    '  html += `<div class="calendar-header">${day}</div>`;\n' +
    '});This adds headers for each day of the week, starting with Monday.8. HTML Generation - Day CellsThe main calendar grid is generated by iterating through each day.let date = startOfWeek;\n' +
    'while (Temporal.PlainDate.compare(date, endOfWeek) <= 0) {\n' +
    '  // ... (logic to determine classes and background color)\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  html += `<div class="${dayClass}" style="background-color: ${isToday ? opts.colors.today : isPast ? opts.colors.past : opts.colors.future};">`;\n' +
    '  html += `<div class="day-number">${date.day}</div>`;\n' +
    '  if (postsByDate[dateKey]) {\n' +
    '    postsByDate[dateKey].forEach(post => {\n' +
    '      // ... (render post links)\n' +
    '    });\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '  html += `</div>`;\n' +
    '  date = date.add({ days: 1 });\n' +
    '}For each day:Determines CSS classes based on whether it’s in the current month, today, past, or futureSets background color accordinglyDisplays the day numberLists any posts for that day with links and file namesAdds content type as an additional CSS class9. Client-Side InteractivityTo enable dynamic navigation without page reloads, the plugin injects a client-side script.const script = `\n' +
    '<script>\n' +
    '(function() {\n' +
    '    function initCalendar() {\n' +
    '        // ... (calendar rendering logic using Temporal)\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    "    if (typeof Temporal !== 'undefined') {\n" +
    '        initCalendar();\n' +
    '    } else {\n' +
    "        if (!document.getElementById('temporal-polyfill')) {\n" +
    "            const script = document.createElement('script');\n" +
    "            script.id = 'temporal-polyfill';\n" +
    "            script.src = 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@js-temporal/polyfill/dist/index.umd.js';\n" +
    '            script.onload = function() {\n' +
    "                document.dispatchEvent(new Event('temporal-loaded'));\n" +
    '            };\n' +
    '            document.head.appendChild(script);\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    "        document.addEventListener('temporal-loaded', initCalendar);\n" +
    '    }\n' +
    '})();\n' +
    '</script>\n' +
    '`;This script:Checks for Temporal Support: It verifies if Temporal is available natively or via an existing polyfill.Dynamic Polyfill Loading: If Temporal is missing, it dynamically loads the polyfill from a CDN.Event Handling: It listens for clicks on “Previous” and “Next” links, updates the state, and re-renders the calendar grid on the client side using the embedded postsData.10. CSS StylingBasic CSS is included inline for the calendar appearance.const css = `\n' +
    '  <style>\n' +
    '    .${opts.calendarClass} {\n' +
    '      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .calendar-grid {\n' +
    '      display: grid;\n' +
    '      grid-template-columns: repeat(7, 1fr);\n' +
    '      gap: 1px;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .calendar-header {\n' +
    '      background-color: ${opts.colors.header};\n' +
    '      padding: 10px;\n' +
    '      text-align: center;\n' +
    '      font-weight: bold;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .${opts.dayClass} {\n' +
    '      min-height: 100px;\n' +
    '      padding: 5px;\n' +
    '      border: 1px solid #ccc;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .other-month {\n' +
    '      opacity: 0.5;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .${opts.postClass} {\n' +
    '      margin: 2px 0;\n' +
    '      font-size: 0.8em;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .filename {\n' +
    '      font-size: 0.7em;\n' +
    '      color: #666;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '    .${opts.navClass} {\n' +
    '      display: flex;\n' +
    '      justify-content: space-between;\n' +
    '      align-items: center;\n' +
    '      margin-bottom: 10px;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '  </style>\n' +
    '`;\n' +
    '\n' +
    'return css + html;This provides basic styling for the calendar layout, colors, and typography.Performance ConsiderationsPost Collection ImpactCollecting all posts into a single array in the plugin has the following performance implications:Memory Usage: Creates a new array with references to all posts from specified collections. Eleventy already holds collections in memory, so this duplicates references. For small sites (<1000 posts), memory impact is negligible; for larger sites, it could increase consumption.Build Time Overhead: The spread operator and array operations add minor computational cost. However, this enables efficient single-pass processing for grouping and rendering.Scalability Concerns: Sites with 10,000+ posts might experience slower builds or higher memory usage. Consider incremental processing or date-based filtering for very large sites.Overall Trade-off: This approach favors simplicity over optimization, suitable for most Eleventy projects. Performance is typically acceptable unless dealing with exceptionally large content volumes.Process Flowflowchart TD\n' +
    '    A[Plugin loads, sets up options] --> B[User calls shortcode in template]\n' +
    '    B --> C[Calculate target month and date range]\n' +
    '    C --> D[Gather posts from configured collections]\n' +
    '    D --> E[Group posts by date]\n' +
    '    E --> F[Generate navigation, headers, and day cells with posts]\n' +
    '    F --> G[Add CSS for appearance]\n' +
    '    G --> H[Return complete HTML/CSS string]The process follows these steps:Initia'... 674 more characters,
  readingTime: { text: '7 min read', minutes: 6.34, time: 380400, words: 1268 }
}
