reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: "While the base concept of Island Architecture focuses on performance, there is a second major benefit: Resilience. By isolating components, we can ensure that if one fails (or isn’t supported by the browser), the rest of the page remains unaffected.This guide demonstrates an advanced pattern for @11ty/is-land: building a relative-date component that relies on the modern (but not yet universal) JavaScript Temporal API. We will implement a “Show on Success” strategy to prevent both layout shifts and broken UI.The Strategy: “Show on Success”Standard hydration often results in a “Flash of Unformatted Content” (FOUC) or “Layout Shift” as static HTML is replaced by a component. The “Show on Success” pattern flips this: the element remains invisible until the JavaScript confirms it can successfully render. This is particularly vital for the Temporal API, which currently lacks universal browser support.CSS (Global or Component-level)We use the :not([ready]) selector to keep the custom element out of the visual flow. By using display: none, we ensure that the element does not occupy space, preventing empty gaps if the browser fails the Temporal support check./* Ensure the island doesn't affect layout until it is fully functional */\n" +
    'relative-date:not([ready]) {\n' +
    '  display: none;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '/* Optional: Add a subtle fade-in effect for a smoother transition */\n' +
    'relative-date[ready] {\n' +
    '  display: inline;\n' +
    '  animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease-in;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '@keyframes fadeIn {\n' +
    '  from { opacity: 0; }\n' +
    '  to { opacity: 1; }\n' +
    '}HTML in EleventyIn your Eleventy templates (Nunjucks or Liquid), you pass the raw data through the cascade. Note that we provide no fallback text inside the relative-date tag. This ensures that if the browser doesn’t support the logic, the user sees nothing rather than an unformatted date string.<is-land on:visible>\n' +
    "  <!-- The 'date' attribute is populated by Eleventy's data cascade -->\n" +
    '  <relative-date date="{{ page.date | htmlDateString }}"></relative-date>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  <template data-island>\n' +
    '    <!-- This script is only loaded when the element becomes visible -->\n' +
    '    <script type="module" src="/js/relative-date.js"></script>\n' +
    '  </template>\n' +
    '</is-land>The Component Script (JavaScript)Save this file as /js/relative-date.js. This script implements the “Swap Strategy,” allowing us to inject a complex, multi-unit time difference into a localized string while maintaining the correct grammar for “ago.”/**\n' +
    ' * relative-date.js\n' +
    ' * Native Temporal Implementation (No Polyfill)\n' +
    ' */\n' +
    'function init() {\n' +
    "  // Exit immediately if the browser doesn't support the new Temporal API\n" +
    '  if (!globalThis.Temporal) {\n' +
    "    console.info('Temporal API not supported. Element will remain hidden.');\n" +
    '    return;\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  /**\n' +
    '   * Calculates and formats the relative date string.\n' +
    '   * Returns null if formatting fails, keeping the element hidden.\n' +
    '   */\n' +
    "  function formatRelativeDate(dateStr, locale = 'en-US') {\n" +
    '    try {\n' +
    '      const postDate = Temporal.PlainDate.from(dateStr);\n' +
    '      const today = Temporal.Now.plainDateISO();\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      // Calculate the difference with years as the largest unit\n' +
    "      const diff = postDate.until(today, { largestUnit: 'year' });\n" +
    '\n' +
    '      // Handle future dates (not expected for blog posts, but good for safety)\n' +
    '      if (diff.sign === -1) {\n' +
    "        return new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat(locale, { numeric: 'auto' }).format(0, 'second');\n" +
    '      }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      const parts = [];\n' +
    '      const years = Math.abs(diff.years);\n' +
    '      const months = Math.abs(diff.months);\n' +
    '      const days = Math.abs(diff.days);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      const formatUnit = (val, unit) =>\n' +
    "        new Intl.NumberFormat(locale, { style: 'unit', unit, unitDisplay: 'long' }).format(val);\n" +
    '\n' +
    "      if (years > 0) parts.push(formatUnit(years, 'year'));\n" +
    "      if (months > 0) parts.push(formatUnit(months, 'month'));\n" +
    "      if (days > 0) parts.push(formatUnit(days, 'day'));\n" +
    '\n' +
    "      // If it's today, return a simple localized string\n" +
    '      if (parts.length === 0) {\n' +
    "        return new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat(locale, { numeric: 'auto' }).format(0, 'day');\n" +
    '      }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      // Combine units (e.g., "1 year and 2 months")\n' +
    "      const listFormatter = new Intl.ListFormat(locale, { style: 'long', type: 'conjunction' });\n" +
    '      const detailedList = listFormatter.format(parts);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      // Use RelativeTimeFormat to get the correct "ago" context for the locale\n' +
    "      const rtf = new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat(locale, { numeric: 'always' });\n" +
    "      const largestUnit = years > 0 ? 'year' : (months > 0 ? 'month' : 'day');\n" +
    '      const largestValue = years > 0 ? years : (months > 0 ? months : days);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      const standardRelative = rtf.format(-largestValue, largestUnit); // e.g., "1 year ago"\n' +
    '      const standardUnit = formatUnit(largestValue, largestUnit);     // e.g., "1 year"\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      // Swap the simple unit with our detailed list\n' +
    '      if (standardRelative.includes(standardUnit)) {\n' +
    '        return standardRelative.replace(standardUnit, detailedList);\n' +
    '      }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      return standardRelative;\n' +
    '    } catch (err) {\n' +
    "      console.error('Relative date calculation error:', err);\n" +
    '      return null;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  class RelativeDate extends HTMLElement {\n' +
    '    connectedCallback() {\n' +
    "      const date = this.getAttribute('date');\n" +
    '      // Automatically detect language from the HTML tag or default to English\n' +
    "      const locale = document.documentElement.lang || 'en-US';\n" +
    '      const formatted = formatRelativeDate(date, locale);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '      if (formatted) {\n' +
    '        this.innerText = formatted;\n' +
    '        // The presence of this attribute triggers the CSS reveal\n' +
    "        this.setAttribute('ready', '');\n" +
    '      }\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '\n' +
    "  if (!customElements.get('relative-date')) {\n" +
    "    customElements.define('relative-date', RelativeDate);\n" +
    '  }\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'init();Resilience and Fallback StrategiesA key tenet of Island Architecture is that the failure of one island should not compromise the integrity of the whole page.The “All or Nothing” Logic for TemporalIn this implementation, we’ve prioritized a clean UI over a degraded one.Initial State: The relative-date tag is rendered by Eleventy, but remains display: none via CSS.Dynamic Loading: When the user scrolls to the island, is-land fetches the script.Graceful Exit: If the script loads but finds no Temporal support (e.g., in an older version of Safari or Chrome), the script silently exits. The element stays display: none.Successful Reveal: Only when the logic produces a valid human-readable string does the ready attribute appear, instructing the browser to finally show the element.Performance Implications & Core Web VitalsUsing islands directly correlates to better performance scores, specifically targeting the metrics that search engines prioritize.Total Blocking Time (TBT)In traditional hydration, the main thread is “blocked” while the browser parses and executes a large bundle. By using on:idle or on:visible, we break up these tasks into smaller chunks, allowing the browser to remain responsive to user scrolls and clicks.Interaction to Next Paint (INP)If a user tries to interact with a page while a massive script is hydrating, the browser will feel sluggish. Islands ensure that interactivity is localized. A script for a relative date won’t interfere with the interactivity of a search bar at the top of the page.Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)Because we use display: none for the initial state of the relative-date component, it doesn’t occupy any height or width in the document flow. When it is revealed, it appears as an inline element, which (if placed correctly within a line of text) results in zero layout shift.Integration ChecklistDependency: Install the component via npm install @11ty/is-land.Asset Management: Ensure your Eleventy build process (like eleventy-plugin-bundle or a simple pass-through) copies the is-land.js and relative-date.js files to your _site directory.Global Styles: Include the :not([ready]) CSS in your main stylesheet to prevent FOUC.Template Wrapping: Audit your templates for non-critical JS and wrap them in <is-land> tags with appropriate triggers. on:visible is usually the safest and most effective default for metadata.',
  readingTime: { text: '6 min read', minutes: 5.385, time: 323100, words: 1077 }
}
