reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'HTMX, or Hypertext Markup Extensions, is a JavaScript library that allows developers to build web applications using HTML instead of JavaScript. It was created by Carson Gross and was initially released on November 24, 2020. You can integrate HTMX into an HTML document by adding a script tag that links to the HTMX library.HTMX extends HTML with custom attributes that allow developers to:Access AJAX, CSS Transitions, WebSockets, and Server-Sent Events directly in HTMLDynamically update web pagesInsert server responses into specific parts of a web page without redrawing the entire pageHTMX uses HTML attributes to:Swap out parts of the user interface with a response from a serverDefine the HTTP method to be used when making a request to the serverSpecify the element on the page where the response from the server will be displayedDetermine what user action will trigger the HTMX requestHTMX allows developers to:Avoid sending too much JavaScript to the browserSimplify the process of building web applicationsWrite code that resembles ordinary HTML code and is easy to understandWorking ExampleThis is a basic example of a Task List application created in HTMX and Javascript. The application allows users to add tasks, mark tasks as complete, and delete tasks.Before we start, there’s a caveat: The application as currently written doesn’t preserve the tasks when the page is reloaded. To save the tasks between page loads, you would need to store them in a database or use local storage, Indexed DB or similar technologies.Specific HTMX attributes used in this example:hx-post: Sends an HTTP POST request to the server at the specified URLhx-on: Defines the event that will trigger the HTMX request. In this case when the AddTask is submitted<form\n' +
    '  class="add-task-form"\n' +
    '  hx-post="#"\n' +
    '  hx-on="submit: addTask(event)">\n' +
    '  <input\n' +
    '    type="text"\n' +
    '    id="task-input"\n' +
    '    placeholder="Enter a new task..." required>\n' +
    '  <button type="submit">Add Task</button>\n' +
    '</form>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<div\n' +
    '  class="task-list"\n' +
    '  id="task-list">\n' +
    '  <!-- Tasks will be dynamically added here -->\n' +
    '</div>The Javascript code is made of three functions:addTask: Adds a new task to the task list. It is triggered when the form is submittedtoggleComplete: Toggles the completed class on a task element. It is triggered when the “Toggle Complete” button is clickeddeleteTask: Deletes a task from the task list. It is triggered when the “Delete” button is clickedThe addTask function first prevents the default form submission behavior, and then gets the task input value, creates a new task element, and appends it to the task list.The toggleComplete function toggles the completed class on the task element, changing its appearance.The deleteTask function uses the hx-delete attribute to send a DELETE request to the server when the delete button is clicked. In this example, the server is the same page, and the task is removed from the DOM.// A variable to keep track of the task ID, incrementing for each new task\n' +
    'let taskId = 0;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '// Add a task to the list\n' +
    'function addTask(event) {\n' +
    '  event.preventDefault();  // Prevent the form from submitting and refreshing the page\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Get the task input element and its trimmed value\n' +
    '  const taskInput = document.getElementById("task-input");\n' +
    '  const taskText = taskInput.value.trim();\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // If the input is empty, exit the function without adding a task\n' +
    '  if (taskText === "") return;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Get the task list element where tasks will be appended\n' +
    '  const taskList = document.getElementById("task-list");\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Increment the task ID for the new task\n' +
    '  taskId++;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Create a new task element as a <div>\n' +
    '  const taskElement = document.createElement("div");\n' +
    '  taskElement.className = "task";  // Assign a class for styling purposes\n' +
    '  taskElement.id = `task-${taskId}`;  // Unique ID for each task using the task ID\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Set the inner HTML of the task element, including task text and buttons\n' +
    '  taskElement.innerHTML = `\n' +
    '    <span>${taskText}</span>\n' +
    '    <!-- Task text displayed -->\n' +
    '    <div>\n' +
    '      <button\n' +
    '        hx-get="#"\n' +
    '        hx-on="click: toggleComplete(\'${taskId}\')"> <!-- Button to toggle the completion state -->\n' +
    '          Toggle Complete\n' +
    '      </button>\n' +
    '      <button class="delete-btn"\n' +
    '        hx-delete="#"\n' +
    '        hx-on="click: deleteTask(\'${taskId}\')"> <!-- Button to delete the task -->\n' +
    '          Delete\n' +
    '      </button>\n' +
    '    </div>\n' +
    '  `;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Add the new task element to the task list\n' +
    '  taskList.appendChild(taskElement);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  // Clear the input field after adding the task\n' +
    '  taskInput.value = "";\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '// Toggle the completion state of a task\n' +
    'function toggleComplete(taskId) {\n' +
    '  // Find the task element using its unique ID\n' +
    '  const taskElement = document.getElementById(`task-${taskId}`);\n' +
    '  // Toggle the "completed" class to change the appearance of the task\n' +
    '  taskElement.classList.toggle("completed");\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    '// Delete a task\n' +
    'function deleteTask(taskId) {\n' +
    '  // Find the task element using its unique ID\n' +
    '  const taskElement = document.getElementById(`task-${taskId}`);\n' +
    '  // Remove the task element from the DOM\n' +
    '  taskElement.remove();\n' +
    '}This example works with an in-memory task list, and the tasks are added, marked as complete, and deleted using HTMX attributes. In a more serious application, you would need to handle these actions on the server side and update the client-side view accordingly.Fetching External DataThis example uses an endpoint from REST Countries to fetch country data and display it on the page.The load-btn button triggers an HTMX request to fetch data from the REST Countries API. The response is an array of country objects, which are then displayed on the page using a template. The instantiation will be done in Javascript.<button\n' +
    '  class="load-btn"\n' +
    '  hx-get="https://restcountries.com/v3.1/all"\n' +
    '  hx-target="#countries-list"\n' +
    '  hx-swap="innerHTML">\n' +
    '    Load Countries Data\n' +
    '</button>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<p>Click the button to fetch and display data about countries.\n' +
    '</p>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<div id="countries-list">\n' +
    '  <em>No countries loaded yet. Click the button above to fetch data.</em>\n' +
    '</div>\n' +
    '<template id="country-card-template">\n' +
    '  <div class="country-card">\n' +
    '    <h3>{{name}}</h3>\n' +
    '    <p><strong>Region:</strong> {{region}}</p>\n' +
    '    <p><strong>Population:</strong> {{population}}</p>\n' +
    '    <p><strong>Capital:</strong> {{capital}}</p>\n' +
    '    <img src="{{flag}}" alt="Flag of {{name}}">\n' +
    '  </div>\n' +
    '</template>The Javascript adds an HTMX-specific event listener (htmx:afterOnLoad) to the document body. This event is triggered after an HTMX request has been completed. The event listener parses the response data, clears the previous results, and populates the countries-list element with the country data using the template.The code checks if the response is empty and returns if there is no data to display. It then iterates over the country data, replacing placeholders in the template with the actual country data. The resulting HTML is appended to the countries-list element.document.body.addEventListener("htmx:afterOnLoad", (event) => {\n' +
    '  const responseData = event.detail.xhr.response;\n' +
    '  if (!responseData) return;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  const countries = JSON.parse(responseData);\n' +
    '\n' +
    "  const countriesContainer = document.getElementById('countries-list');\n" +
    '\n' +
    "  const template = document.getElementById('country-card-template').innerHTML;\n" +
    '\n' +
    '  countriesContainer.innerHTML = "";\n' +
    '\n' +
    '  countries.forEach(country => {\n' +
    '    const cardHtml = template\n' +
    `      .replace('{{name}}', country.name?.common || "N/A")\n` +
    `      .replace('{{region}}', country.region || "N/A")\n` +
    "      .replace('{{population}}', country.population.toLocaleString())\n" +
    `      .replace('{{capital}}', country.capital ? country.capital[0] : "N/A")\n` +
    `      .replace('{{flag}}', country.flags?.png || "");\n` +
    '\n' +
    '    countriesContainer.innerHTML += cardHtml;\n' +
    '  });\n' +
    '});One final detail that may be interesting is that we can use from in the hx-trigger attribute to target a different element than the one that triggered the event. For example, if we want to trigger an HTMX request when a user types in an input field, we can use the from attribute to specify the input field.Links and ResourcesHTMX — high power tools for HTMLWhat is HTMX? Why it Matters? and How to use it.',
  readingTime: { text: '6 min read', minutes: 5.46, time: 327600, words: 1092 }
}
