reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'Bind, call and apply are old concepts in Javascript. They are likely the subject of many interview questions, blog posts and tutorials but I still find it hard to understand these functions, how they work and how they are different from each other.This post is my attempt at understanding and explaining how these concepts work.General observationsThese functions provide a basic level of composability to Javascript.They perform the same task (creating a bound function) in different ways.BindBind lets you specify the object that this references inside the bound function.The simplest use of bind() is to make a function that, no matter how it is called, is called with a particular this value.A common mistake I’ve made (and sometimes still do) is to extract a method from an object, and then call that function outside the original object and expect it to use the original object as its this reference.If we’re not careful the original object is usually lost. Creating a bound function using the original object, solves this problem.To fully understand what this means and what bind() does we’ll look at two examples:The first example runs through basic usage of bind() in a bound function.We create a variable bound to this and a value for x in the global scope. The top-level ‘this’ is bound to ‘globalThis’ in scripts.We also create a module with a property x and a method getX() that returns the value of x.this.x = 9;\n' +
    'const module = {\n' +
    '  x: 81,\n' +
    '  getX() {\n' +
    '    return this.x;\n' +
    '  },\n' +
    '};The first step is to create a bound variable. module.getX() will produce 81; the value of X inside the module.console.log(module.getX()); // 81retrieveX references the module’s getX() function. However, since we’re not making an explicit reference, this is bound to the global object and will produce a different result.const retrieveX = module.getX;\n' +
    'console.log(retrieveX()); // 9The ‘boundGetX’ function binds the this parameter bound to ‘module’ so, when we call it, we get the value of x inside the module.const boundGetX = retrieveX.bind(module);\n' +
    'console.log(boundGetX()); // 81The second example shows how to make a function with pre-specified initial arguments.We create two functions.list() will list the arguments it passes.addArguments() takes two arguments and will return the sum of the arguments.function list(...args) {\n' +
    '  return args;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'function addArguments(arg1, arg2) {\n' +
    '  return arg1 + arg2;\n' +
    '}We test the functions to make sure that they work as expected and produce a list of the arguments and the addition of the two passed arguments.console.log(list(1, 2, 3)); // [1, 2, 3]\n' +
    '\n' +
    'console.log(addArguments(1, 2)); // 3// Create a function with a preset leading argumentThe next two definitions use null as the value of this. This will bind the function to the global object.If we don’t provide a value then leadingTenList will just return 10.If we provide one or more arguments then leadingTenList will return a list starting with 10 and followed by the arguments in the order they were defined.const leadingTenList = list.bind(null, 10);console.log(leadingTenList()); // [10]\n' +
    'console.log(leadingTenList(1, 2, 3)); // [10, 1, 2, 3]The addTen function will return the sum of 10 plus the argument. If we don’t provide an argument then it will return 10.const addTen = addArguments.bind(null, 10);The function expects a single argument. If you pass it then it will add 10 to it and return the result.If you pass more arguments than those in the function the additional arguments will be ignored.console.log(addTen(5)); // 15\n' +
    'console.log(addTen(5, 10)); // 15CallCall calls the bound function with a given this value and arguments provided individually.We first define our base module with all the properties and methods that we want to share.function Car(model, year, doors) {\n' +
    '  this.model = model;\n' +
    '  this.year = year;\n' +
    '  this.doors = doors;\n' +
    '}We then define a more specialised object. We use Car.call() to bring the properties of the Car object into the Ferrari object along with custom attributes exclusive to the derived object.function Ferrari(year, doors) {\n' +
    "  Car.call(this, 'F30', year, doors);\n" +
    '  this.onSale = false;\n' +
    '}console.log(new Ferrari(2015, 2));\n' +
    '\n' +
    'Ferrari {\n' +
    "  model: 'F30',\n" +
    '  year: 2015,\n' +
    '  doors: 2,\n' +
    '  onSale: false\n' +
    '}ApplyApply calls this function with a given this value, and arguments provided as an array (or an array-like object).As usual, we first define the array that we want to work with.const numbers = [5, 6, 2, 3, 7];Then we define two function literals. When we use apply() rather than call() because we have an array of values that we want to work with.const max = Math.max.apply(null, numbers);\n' +
    '\n' +
    'const min = Math.min.apply(null, numbers);\n' +
    '\n' +
    'console.log(max); // 7\n' +
    'console.log(min); // 2There are array and string methods that would achieve similar results. But this is still available and you’re likely to get called out on it during interviews so it’s better to know the differences.Links and ResourcesUnderstanding Call, Bind and Apply Methods in JavaScriptMDNBindCallApply',
  readingTime: { text: '4 min read', minutes: 3.87, time: 232200, words: 774 }
}
