reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: "Looping through a flat JSON file is easy, doing the same through a nested JSON object, is not so easy.There is no built-in way to loop through nested arrays of JSON properties so we have to develop our own strategies.The solution is encapsulated in the logJSON function. It does the following:Check if the JSON input is null or undefined. If it is, then return, there’s nothing to doCheck the type of the incoming JSON object using the typeof operator. We will use this check laterIf the object is a number, a string or a boolean valueIf the items is not an object then we throw an error, it’s not an array or an objectThe final check is and if/else block for arrays and objects. In both these instances we recursively call logJSON to process the children elementsfunction logJSON(json, indent = '') {\n" +
    '    // 1\n' +
    '    if (json === null || json === undefined) {\n' +
    '      console.log(`${indent}null`);\n' +
    '      return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 2\n' +
    '    const jsonType = typeof json;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 3\n' +
    "    if (jsonType === 'string' || jsonType === 'number' || jsonType === 'boolean') {\n" +
    '        console.log(`${indent}${JSON.stringify(json)}`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 4\n' +
    "    if (jsonType !== 'object') {\n" +
    '        console.error(`${indent}Error: Unsupported data type`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 5\n' +
    '    if (Array.isArray(json)) {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}[`);\n' +
    '        json.forEach((value, index) => {\n' +
    '            console.log(`${indent}  [${index}]`);\n' +
    "            logJSON(value, indent + '    ');\n" +
    '        });\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}]`);\n' +
    '    } else {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}{`);\n' +
    '        for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(json)) {\n' +
    '            console.log(`${indent}  "${key}":`);\n' +
    "            logJSON(value, indent + '    ');\n" +
    '        }\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}}`);\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    "}The code should work well, but there’s an unsolved issue with it.As written, the code doesn’t handle circular references in the JSON file.To handle detecting circular references, I’ve made the following changes.create a new set and call it visited. I chose to use a set because it will only store unique values, there can’t be any duplicates.For every element, we add it to the visited Set. Once we’re done processing the node, we remove it from the set to make sure that we can process other instances of the same element later.function logJSON(json, indent = '', visited = new Set()) {\n" +
    '    if (json === null || json === undefined) {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}null`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    const jsonType = typeof json;\n' +
    '\n' +
    "    if (jsonType === 'string' || jsonType === 'number' || jsonType === 'boolean') {\n" +
    '        console.log(`${indent}${JSON.stringify(json)}`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    "    if (jsonType !== 'object') {\n" +
    '        console.error(`${indent}Error: Unsupported data type`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // Handling circular references\n' +
    '    if (visited.has(json)) {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}Circular reference detected`);\n' +
    '        return;\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // Mark the current object as visited\n' +
    '    visited.add(json);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    if (Array.isArray(json)) {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}Array: [`);\n' +
    '        json.forEach((value, index) => {\n' +
    '            console.log(`${indent}  [${index}]`);\n' +
    "            logJSON(value, indent + '    ', visited);\n" +
    '        });\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}]`);\n' +
    '    } else {\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}Object: {`);\n' +
    '        for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(json)) {\n' +
    '            console.log(`${indent}  "${key}":`);\n' +
    "            logJSON(value, indent + '    ', visited);\n" +
    '        }\n' +
    '        console.log(`${indent}}`);\n' +
    '    }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // Unmark the current object after processing to allow re-visiting in other branches\n' +
    '    visited.delete(json);\n' +
    '}There is a lot of code to do this but it should work for most nested JSON code.For simplicity’s sake, I chose to log the output to console. In production code we’ll definitely want to do something more useful :).',
  readingTime: { text: '3 min read', minutes: 2.475, time: 148500, words: 495 }
}
