reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'One of the reasons to move my blog from WordPress to Eleventy was the flexibility of convert the Markdown I write into proper HTML without using raw HTML in the Markdown files.But adding Markdown-it plugins mean additional syntax to remember and different ways to use established Markdown-it syntax.This document is my attempt at remembering all the things I’ve added so I can continue to use them.This post is my attempt at a cheat sheet for the extra Markdown Elements that I use in this blog.!!! tip This post is specific to this site\n' +
    'The commands, as explained in this post, are designed for this blog and will only work as explained here.If you want to get them to work in your own site contact me and I’ll try to help.\n' +
    '!!!Front matter attributesI don’t need video embeds, Mermaid or Mavo on every page so I don’t load them. I use conditional nunjuck tags to only load them if there is a matching tag in the post’s front matter.For example, the tags supporting lite-youtube video embeds will only load if the front matter for the post has a youtube attribute set to trueA front matter declaration supporting all the conditional loads would look like this:---\n' +
    'title: "My Markdown cheatsheet"\n' +
    'date: "2023-10-15"\n' +
    'youtube: true\n' +
    'vimeo: true\n' +
    'mermaid: true\n' +
    'mavo: true\n' +
    '---Handling drafts and future postingsOne thing that has been very frustrating is to figure out how to handle drafts and future posts (posts that are complete but will not be published for a while).if you set draft: true in the post front matter or the date is in the future from the date you’re publishing. Eleventy will set the permalink to false and exclude the post from all collectionsAttributesThis plugin will insert the specified attributes to the element. If the class exists the look of the content may change.For example:The following Markdown code:column1 | column2\n' +
    '--- | --- |\n' +
    'Read | Write\n' +
    '\n' +
    '{.special}Will insert the .special class into the table. Inspect the code to see the result.column1column2ReadWrite{.special}While the Markdown below will change the markers to use a customized CSS list number style.1. 22354\n' +
    '2. 54252\n' +
    '3. 1231231542\n' +
    '\n' +
    '{.custom-ordered}22354542521231231542{.custom-ordered}FiguresUsing the figure plugin. Eleventy will generate full figure elements with a caption using the figcaption element.The following Markdown code:![caption and alt text](url/or/path/to/the/image.ext)Will produce this HTML:<figure>\n' +
    '  <img src="url/or/path/to/the/image.ext"\n' +
    '    alt="caption and alt text"\n' +
    '    loading="lazy"\n' +
    '    decoding="async"\n' +
    '    class="lazy">\n' +
    '  <figcaption>caption and alt text</figcaption>\n' +
    '</figure>This will handle both native lazy loading and third-party lazy-loading scripts that require a class attribute to be present for them to work.Also note the use of the decoding property to load the image in prallel with the rest of the page so text may render before images have finished loading… score!One drawback is that, as configured, the plugin will use the same text for both the alt attribute and the content of the figcaption element. This is likely not a good idea but I’m still working on possible solutionsDefinition listsDefinition lists consist of two parts wrapped in a list definition (dl), the term (using the dt element) and one or more definitions (using the dd element).The Markdown version looks like this:term1\n' +
    ': First paragraph of the definition\n' +
    ': Second paragraph of the definition\n' +
    '\n' +
    'term2\n' +
    ': First paragraph of the definition\n' +
    ': Second paragraph of the definitionIt will produce the following HTML code:<dl>\n' +
    '  <dt>Term1</dt>\n' +
    '  <dd>First paragraph of the definition</dd>\n' +
    '  <dd>Second paragraph of the definition<dd>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<dt>Term2</dt>\n' +
    '  <dd>First paragraph of the definition</dd>\n' +
    '  <dd>Second paragraph of the definition<dd>\n' +
    '</dl>term1First paragraph of the definitionSecond paragraph of the definitionterm2First paragraph of the definitionSecond paragraph of the definitionYou can have multiple definitions for a term and there is no length limit for each definitionkbd elementskbd represents keyboard input, for example key combinations to run a command.Run [[cmd]] + [[shift]] + [[R]] on macOS\n' +
    '\n' +
    'Run [[ctrl]] + [[shift]] + [[R]] on Windows.Will produce the following HTML<p>Run <kbd>⌘</kbd> + <kbd>⇧</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd> on macOS</p>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<p>Run <kbd>⌃</kbd> + <kbd>⇧</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd> on Windows to reload the browser window.</p>Run [[cmd]] + [[shift]] + [[R]] on macOSRun [[ctrl]] + [[shift]] + [[R]] onThe kbd plugin will convert some words into symbols. cmd is transofrmed to ⌘, shift to ⇧ and ctrl to ^.I’ll evaluate if this is still what I want after I’ve had the chance to run it for a while.FootnotesFootnotes are made of two parts, the callout in the text and the footnote text.The callout is surrounded by square brakets with a ^ and the marker that we want to use. For example [^1] is a valid markerThe footnote text begins with the same callout that we used in the text followed by a colon, like this [^1]:, followed by one or more paragraphs of text.It doesn’t matter where we put the foonote, it will be moved to the end of the document and displayed in sequential oder.The text that needs a footnote goes here. [^1]\n' +
    '\n' +
    '[^1]: This is the text of the footnote.The punctuation is important. If you miss the colon on the footer text then the footnote will be displayed as is. This is definitely not what you want.The text that needs a footnote goes here. Look for the footnote at the bottom of the page. This is the text of the footnote.AdmonitionsAdmonitions are asides with relevant information that are not essential to the body of the text.The following types of admonitions are supported:infonotetipwarningbugdangerfailuresuccessThe structure is the same:Three exclamation marks, !!! followed by the type of admonition and an optional titleIf you don’t provide a title, then the type of admonition will be usedThe body of the admonitionThree exclamation marks, !!! on a line of their ownA warning admonition would look like this!!! warning\n' +
    "You never know who'll get to read it :)\n" +
    '!!!<div class="admonition warning">\n' +
    '  <p class="admonition-title">warning</p>\n' +
    '  <p>It works so much better when you do your research before engaging in arguments</p>\n' +
    '</div>!!! warning\n' +
    'You never know who’ll get to read it :)\n' +
    '!!!You can also add a title to the admonition by adding the title after the type of admonition.To add a title to the warning example, the modified code will look like this:!!! warning Do your research\n' +
    "You never know who'll get to read it :)\n" +
    '!!!And the resulting HTML will look like this:<div class="admonition warning">\n' +
    '  <p class="admonition-title">Do your research</p>\n' +
    "  <p>You never know who'll get to read it :)</p>\n" +
    '</div>!!! warning Do your research\n' +
    'You never know who’ll get to read it :)\n' +
    '!!!New style of admonitionsThere are times when I want a different type of admonitions.  Instead of modifying the Markdown plugin, I decided to create a web component based on  Lea Verou’s admonition design on her website (https://lea.verou.me/).The admonition takes these attributes: type, no-icon, and title.The custom-admonition component supports the following types of admonitions:admonition (plain color, no icon, no title)notewarninginfotipdangertodoIf the no-icon attribute is present, the admonition will not display an icon.If the title attribute is present, the admonition will display the title instead of the default title for the type of admonition.<custom-admonition plain>\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a plain admonition without an icon</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="warning" title="Do your research">\n' +
    "\t<p>You never know who'll get to read it :)</p>\n" +
    '\t<p>Warning admonition with a custom title and an icon</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="note" no-icon>\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a note admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="warning">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a warning admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="info">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is an info admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="tip">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a tip admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="danger">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a danger admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<custom-admonition type="todo">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a todo admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>It produces the following result:<custom-admonition plain>\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a plain admonition without an icon</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="warning" title="Do your research">\n' +
    "\t<p>You never know who'll get to read it :)</p>\n" +
    '\t<p>Warning admonition with a custom title and an icon</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="note" no-icon>\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a note admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="warning">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a warning admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="info">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is an info admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="tip">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a tip admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="danger">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a danger admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition><custom-admonition type="todo">\n' +
    '\t<p>This is a todo admonition with an icon and default title</p>\n' +
    '</custom-admonition>lite-youtube and lite-vimeolite-youtube and lite-vimeo are not Markdown and require Javascript to run, but they will improve the performance of pages.Each of these custom elements reduce the number of HTTP requests the browser makes to render the video.Because I’m adding the scrips to the rendered pages, I’ve followed the suggestions in Efficiently load Javascript with defer and async and place the scripts in the head of the page and add the defer attribute.YouTubeThe lite-youtube custom element embeds a YouTube embedded iframe.It takes videoid attribute'... 5693 more characters,
