reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'Partial application and currying are two techniquest to make functions more flexible and reusable by enabling composition (building complex functions from simpler ones).If you come from a functional programming background you may be familiar with these techniques.While we have vanilla Javascript functions to do this, sometimes it may be easier to use a library like lodash to do this, particularly if you already use it for other purposes.Partial applicationPartial application allows us to fix a function’s arguments. This lets us derive new functions, with specific behavior, from other, more general functions.At the most basic level a partial application functions takes a function and one or more arguments. It returns a new function that calls the original function with all of the arguments.function partialApply(fn, ...args) {\n' +
    '  return fn.bind(null, ...args);\n' +
    '}so if we have a function like this:function add(a, b) {\n' +
    '  return a + b;\n' +
    '}CurryingCurrying transforms a function that accepts multiple arguments “all at once” into a series of function calls, each of which involves only one argument at a time. Curried functions with a well-designed argument order are convenient to partially apply.Using lodash/fp to create partial functionsLinks and ResourcesPartial Application in JavaScriptPartial Function Application in JavaScript and FlowHow to use partial application to improve your JavaScript codeJavaScript Functional Programming Explained: Partial Application and CurryingLodashFunctional LodashFunctions/MethodscurrycurryRightpartialpartialRight',
  readingTime: { text: '2 min read', minutes: 1.075, time: 64500, words: 215 }
}
