reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'For the most part opening and reading files synchronously in the browser is not a big deal since the files are seldom large enough to cause any performance issues and reading from the local file system is faster than downloading from the web or external resources.When working in certain types of applications it may be better to work with streams since we can’t know how large the files users will upload to the application are and that may block the main thread can cause perceived performance issues.This post will revisit the file system access API code from a previous post use streams to read and write from and to the file system.Getting ReadyBefore we write the code we need to initialize the file handle variable to the empty value.let fileHandle;We also need to set up the HTML elements that will be used to interact with the file system.<button id="openButton">Open</button>\n' +
    '<button id="saveButton">Save</button>\n' +
    '<button id="saveAsButton">Save As</button>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<textarea id="content"></textarea>The IDs for these elements will be referenced in the Javascript code.Read/Open FileWe will use the openFile function to open a file, read its contents and display the content in the textarea element. The function will:Open the file picker and get the file handleGet a readable stream for the fileUse a reader to read the streamUse a do…while loop to read the stream in chunks as long as the stream is not doneWe could also use a while loop to read the stream, but that would require us to set the done variable to false before the loop starts, the do...while loop will run at least once, regardless of the value of doneDisplay the file contents in the textareaCatch any errors that occur during the processThe main difference in this function is that we explicitly use streams to read the file contents.async function openFile() {\n' +
    '  try {\n' +
    '    // 1\n' +
    '\t\t// Open file picker and get file handle\n' +
    '    [fileHandle] = await window.showOpenFilePicker(pickerOpts);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 2\n' +
    '\t\t// Get a readable stream for the file\n' +
    '    const file = await fileHandle.getFile();\n' +
    '    const readableStream = file.stream();\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 3\n' +
    '\t\t// Use a reader to read the stream\n' +
    '    const reader = readableStream.getReader();\n' +
    "    let contents = '';\n" +
    '    let done, value;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '\t\t// 4\n' +
    '\t\t// Loop through the chunks of the\n' +
    '\t\t// stream and add them to the\n' +
    '\t\t// `contents` variable\n' +
    '    do {\n' +
    '      ({ done, value } = await reader.read());\n' +
    '      if (value) {\n' +
    '        contents += new TextDecoder().decode(value);\n' +
    '      }\n' +
    '    } while (!done);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 5\n' +
    '\t\t// Display the file contents in the textarea\n' +
    '    document.getElementById("content").value = contents;\n' +
    '  } catch (err) { // 6\n' +
    '    console.error("Error opening file: ", err.message);\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '}Write/Save File: SaveThe saveFile function will save the contents of the textarea to the the same file that was opened witht the openFile function.The function will:Check if a file has been opened and bail out and throw and alert if no file has been openedInside a try blockGet the contents from the textarea and assign it to the contents variableCreate a writable streamWrite the contents to fileClose the writerCatch any errors that occur during the process in the catch blockasync function saveFile() {\n' +
    '\t// 1\n' +
    '  if (!fileHandle) {\n' +
    '    alert("No file opened yet. Please open a file first.");\n' +
    '    return;\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '  try { // 2 try block\n' +
    '    // 3\n' +
    '\t\t// Get the contents from the textarea\n' +
    '    const contents = document.getElementById("content").value;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 4\n' +
    '\t\t// Create a writable stream\n' +
    '    const writable = await fileHandle.createWritable();\n' +
    '    const writer = writable.getWriter();\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 5\n' +
    '\t\t// Write the contents in chunks\n' +
    '    const encoder = new TextEncoder();\n' +
    '    const encodedContents = encoder.encode(contents);\n' +
    '    await writer.write(encodedContents);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 6\n' +
    '\t\t// Close the writer\n' +
    '    await writer.close();\n' +
    '    alert("File saved successfully!");\n' +
    '  } catch (err) { // 7\n' +
    '    console.error("Error saving file: ", err.message);\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '}Write/Save File: Save AsThe saveAs function will save the contents of the textarea to a file. The difference from the saveFile function is that saveAs will give you the option to change the name of the file you save to.The function will:Show the save file pickerGet the contents from the textarea and assign it to the contents variableCreate a writable stream and a writer for the writable streamWrite the contents to the fileClose the writerUpdate the global file handleCatch any errors that occur during the processasync function saveAs() {\n' +
    '  try {\n' +
    '    // 1\n' +
    '\t\t// Show save file picker\n' +
    '    const newFileHandle = await window.showSaveFilePicker(pickerOpts);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 2\n' +
    '\t\t// Get the contents from the textarea\n' +
    '    const contents = document.getElementById("content").value;\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 3\n' +
    '\t\t// Create a writable stream\n' +
    '    const writable = await newFileHandle.createWritable();\n' +
    '    const writer = writable.getWriter();\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 4\n' +
    '\t\t// Write the contents in chunks\n' +
    '    const encoder = new TextEncoder();\n' +
    '    const encodedContents = encoder.encode(contents);\n' +
    '    await writer.write(encodedContents);\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 5\n' +
    '\t\t// Close the writer\n' +
    '    await writer.close();\n' +
    '    alert("File saved successfully!");\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    // 6\n' +
    '\t\t// Update the global file handle\n' +
    '    fileHandle = newFileHandle;\n' +
    '  } catch (err) { // 7\n' +
    '    console.error("Error saving file as: ", err.message);\n' +
    '  }\n' +
    '}Tyding up: Adding event listenersThe last remaining task is to attach click event listeners to the appropriate buttons.document\n' +
    '  .getElementById("openButton")\n' +
    '  .addEventListener("click", openFile);\n' +
    '\n' +
    'document\n' +
    '  .getElementById("saveButton")\n' +
    '  .addEventListener("click", saveFile);\n' +
    '\n' +
    'document\n' +
    '  .getElementById("saveAsButton")\n' +
    '  .addEventListener("click", saveAs);',
  readingTime: { text: '5 min read', minutes: 4.055, time: 243300, words: 811 }
}
