reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: '!!! warning Warning:\n' +
    'These components are under development and they may change without notice.If the current version of the components meets your needs you may want to pin to the specific version you’re currently using. This may also mean hosting your own version of the library\n' +
    '!!!Working in blog updates (and ideas for future projects) I started looking at web components as an alternative to manually updating code for each type of component I want.These are likely not the only options for what I want to do but are the one I’m evaluating at the time of writing.color.jsColor.js elements provide experimental, ready-made components to address color related code examples.I don’t normally work with colors but it’s always nice to have when explaining different color spaces and when working in color-based demonstrations.Using The ComponentsThe library makes available a series of components so we’ll look at four specific elements. You can review the other components in the websitecolor-pickerThe color-picker elements displays a slider-based color picker that will display the selected color. It will also fire events that will enable you to use the color you picked in styles for other parts of the page.This code:<color-picker space="oklch" color="oklch(60% 30% 180)"></color-picker>Will produce the following result:<color-picker space="oklch" color="oklch(60% 30% 180)"></color-picker>The color-picker reference for the component’s API.color-scaleThe color-scale component provides a visual representation of a color scale presented in the colors attribute.You can enter the colors in any color space supported in color.js. The component will convert the colors to the space specified in the space attribute.This code:<color-scale space="oklch" colors="\n' +
    '  Gray 50: #f9fafb,\n' +
    '  Gray 100: #f3f4f6,\n' +
    '  Gray 200: #e5e7eb,\n' +
    '  Gray 300: #d1d5db,\n' +
    '  Gray 400: #9ca3af,\n' +
    '  Gray 500: #6b7280,\n' +
    '  Gray 600: #4b5563,\n' +
    '  Gray 700: #374151,\n' +
    '  Gray 800: #1f2937,\n' +
    '  Gray 850: #1a202c,\n' +
    '\tGray 900: #111827,\n' +
    '"></color-scale>Will produce the following result:<color-scale space="oklch" colors="\n' +
    'Gray 50: #f9fafb,\n' +
    'Gray 100: #f3f4f6,\n' +
    'Gray 200: #e5e7eb,\n' +
    'Gray 300: #d1d5db,\n' +
    'Gray 400: #9ca3af,\n' +
    'Gray 500: #6b7280,\n' +
    'Gray 600: #4b5563,\n' +
    'Gray 700: #374151,\n' +
    'Gray 800: #1f2937,\n' +
    'Gray 850: #1a202c,\n' +
    ' Gray 900: #111827\n' +
    '"></color-scale>There is no actual API since this is a presentational element but you can check the color-scale page for more information on what attributes are available.color-swatchThe color-swatch element creates color swatches that can be edited if so configured.The input file inside the color-swatch component will render the value of the selected color.This code:<color-swatch value="oklch(70% 0.25 138)" size="large">\n' +
    '\t<input />\n' +
    '</color-swatch>Will render the following result:<color-swatch value="oklch(70% 0.25 138)" size="large">\n' +
    '\t<input />\n' +
    '</color-swatch>color-inlineThe color-swatch element renders a swatch of color inline with other content like text.<p>This will render the color\n' +
    '\t<color-inline contentEditable>lch(50% 40 30)</color-inline> inline.</p><p>This will render the color <color-inline contentEditable>lch(50% 40 30)</color-inline> inline.</p>See the color-inline reference for the component’s API.Loading The LibraryIn your root template, add the following code to conditionally load the library if the variable exists (and it’s set to true).{% raw %}{% if colorjs %}\n' +
    '  <script src="https://elements.colorjs.io/index.js"></script>\n' +
    '{% endif %}{% endraw %}And then, for each individual post add the following code to the post’s front matter.colorjs: trueThis is just one example of how to use web components in an Eleventy blog or application. There are other sets of components that I would like to use and will require similar',
  readingTime: { text: '3 min read', minutes: 2.59, time: 155400, words: 518 }
}
