reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'I’ve always wanted to avoid manually doing data replacement and numbering if I can avoid it.We’ll add counters for the following:Chapters defined by <h2> elementsSections defined by <h3> elementsFigures will be tied to chaptersTables will be tied to chaptersWe’ll work with the headings first and then add whatever we need to so that figures and tables work as designed.Chapters, sections, and subsectionThe basic structure of the styles is the same: If needed we reset counters using counter-reset and increase the appropriate counters using counter-increment.We then use the ::before pseudo-element to write the counter the way we want it to appear on the page.h2 {\n' +
    '  counter-reset: chapter, section, subsection;\n' +
    '  counter-increment: chapter;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'h2::before {\n' +
    "  content: 'Chapter ' counter(chapter) ': ';\n" +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'h3 {\n' +
    '  counter-reset: section, subsection;\n' +
    '  counter-increment: section;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'h3::before {\n' +
    "  content: 'Section ' counter(chapter) '.' counter(section) ' ';\n" +
    '}Figures and tablesWe only need to change one thing in our previous code to make it work with tables and figures.In the counter-reset for chapters, we need to add figures and tablesh2 {\n' +
    '  counter-reset: chapter, section, subsection, figures, tables;\n' +
    '  counter-increment: chapter;\n' +
    '}The figure and table rules are similar to what we already cover. The figure and table elements increase the counters and figcaption and caption use ::before to display the counter text and valuesfigure {\n' +
    '  counter-increment: figures;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'figcaption::before {\n' +
    "  content: 'Figure ' counter(chapter) '-' counter(figures) ': ';\n" +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'table {\n' +
    '  counter-increment: tables;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'caption::before {\n' +
    "  content: 'Table ' counter(chapter) '-' counter(tables) ': ';\n" +
    '}I realize that this is cumbersome but, as with many things in CSS, we trade complexity for Flexibility.We can change the type of counters to uppercase Roman characters by changing the declaration of the counter to make it match the numbering schema that we need.In the example below, we’ve changed the numbering schema to use an Arabic number for chapters and lowercase Roman numerals for the tables. The example would look Table 2-A:table {\n' +
    '  counter-increment: tables;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    'caption::before {\n' +
    "  content: 'Table ' counter(chapter) \n" +
    "  '-' counter(tables, upper-alpha) ': ';\n" +
    '}',
  readingTime: { text: '2 min read', minutes: 1.655, time: 99300, words: 331 }
}
