reading-time plugin: {
  textOnPage: 'Styling the elementInsert the following CSS in the style declaration for our pattern-figure element:host {\n' +
    '    display: block;\n' +
    '    clear: both;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host > figure {\n' +
    '    max-width: min-content;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host > figure > img {\n' +
    '    max-width: inherit\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host > figure > figcaption {\n' +
    '    color: #999;\n' +
    '    font-size: 80%;\n' +
    '    text-align: left;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([align="left"]) > figure  {\n' +
    '    clear: both;\n' +
    '    float: left;\n' +
    '    margin: 2em 0;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([align="center"]) > figure {\n' +
    '    clear: both;\n' +
    '    margin: 2em auto;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([align="right"]) > figure {\n' +
    '    clear: both;\n' +
    '    float: right;\n' +
    '    margin: 2em 0;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([align="left"]) > figure > figcaption,\n' +
    ':host([align="center"]) > figure > figcaption,\n' +
    ':host([align="right"]) > figure > figcaption {\n' +
    '    text-align: left\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host(.code-list) > figure > figcaption {\n' +
    '    border-top: 1px solid black;\n' +
    '    margin-top: 2em;\n' +
    '    color: #999;\n' +
    '    text-align: left;\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([float = "left"]) {\n' +
    '    margin-right: 2rem;\n' +
    '    text-align: left\n' +
    '}\n' +
    '\n' +
    ':host([float = "right"]) {\n' +
    '    margin-left: 2rem;\n' +
    '    text-align: left\n' +
    '}\n' +
    'The :host() pseudo element represents our element, pattern-figure in this case. It allow me to style the element from the element declaration itself and not loose the style encapsulation. It also allows me to create different versions of the element depending on what attributes we use.All the styling is done inside the element itself. TSo what does this all mean in practice? We can create themes for our molecules and change them either with classes or attributes on our elements. For example each of the pattern-figure elements will be styles different, but all the captions will be styled the same way.<pattern-figure class="figure" align="left">\n' +
    '    <figure>\n' +
    '        <img src="/images/IMG_0212.PNG">\n' +
    '        <figcaption>Magazine spread to model after </figcaption>\n' +
    '    </figure>\n' +
    '</pattern-figure>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<pattern-figure class="figure" align="center">\n' +
    '    <figure>\n' +
    '        <img src="/images/IMG_0212.PNG">\n' +
    '        <figcaption>Magazine spread to model after</figcaption>\n' +
    '    </figure>\n' +
    '</pattern-figure>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<pattern-figure class="figure" align="right">\n' +
    '    <figure>\n' +
    '        <img src="/images/IMG_0212.PNG">\n' +
    '        <figcaption>Magazine spread to model after</figcaption>\n' +
    '    </figure>\n' +
    '</pattern-figure>When we need to change the styles of our elements we need to change the definitions on the CSS portion of the element.Custom CSS mixins to create themesRther than define individual properties for each theme we want to implement for our pattern library it may be easier, less tedious and error prone to let a library author to define a mixin, a set of CSS properties as a single custom propertyUsing an extension to the shim that provides custom properties, we can then apply all properties in the mixin to a specific CSS rule in an element’s local DOM. The capability is analogous to var and custom properties, but which allows an entire set of properties to be mixed in.Defining a mixin is just like defining a custom property, but the value is an object that defines one or more rules.selector {\n' +
    '  --mixin-name: {\n' +
    '    /* rules */\n' +
    '  };\n' +
    '}The extension adheres to the CSS @apply rule proposal. Use @apply to apply a mixin like this:@apply(--mixin-name);Mixins can be as simple as defining a set of properties common to one or more versions of the same element or as a theme container in a master css-only element that gets imported and included in each element we want to use the common CSS in.In the pattern-message element demo below we define a set of common attributes for all our message elements as the --pattern-message-theme mixin in the :host selector and then, for the more specific classes we add any additional rules that are specific to the subclass of the pattern we want to modify, mostly adding different background colors to the different types of messages available.<link rel="import" href="../../bower_components/polymer/polymer.html">\n' +
    '\n' +
    '<dom-module id="pattern-message">\n' +
    '    <style is="custom-style">\n' +
    '        /* Apply custom theme to messages */\n' +
    '        :host {\n' +
    '            --pattern-message-theme: {\n' +
    '                border-radius: 1em;\n' +
    '                border: 2px solid black;\n' +
    '                display: block;\n' +
    '                padding: 1em;\n' +
    '                width: 80%;\n' +
    '                margin: 2em auto;\n' +
    '            };\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '        :host(.message) {\n' +
    '            @apply(--pattern-message-theme);\n' +
    '            background-color: lightgrey;\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '        :host(.message-info) {\n' +
    '            @apply(--pattern-message-theme);\n' +
    '            background-color: lightblue;\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '        :host(.message-warning) {\n' +
    '            @apply(--pattern-message-theme);\n' +
    '            background-color: lightyellow;\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    '\n' +
    '        :host(.message-danger) {\n' +
    '            @apply(--pattern-message-theme);\n' +
    '            background-color: indianred;\n' +
    '        }\n' +
    '    </style>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    <template>\n' +
    '        <div>\n' +
    '            <div class="content-wrapper"><content></content></div>\n' +
    '        </div>\n' +
    '    </template>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '    <script>\n' +
    '        Polymer({\n' +
    "            is: 'pattern-message',\n" +
    '\n' +
    '            properties: {}\n' +
    '        });\n' +
    '    </script>\n' +
    '\n' +
    '</dom-module>Templates and PagesAnother aspect of web components that I find very attractive is how easy it is to compose objects using the element’s local DOM and the light DOM for the element as it is written in the host document.<pattern-header></pattern-header>This version of the element will display the object as defined in the template associated to the molecule. It works perfectly when we want to demonstrate what each smaller molecule looks like. We can further customize it by assining CSS classesDepending on how detailed you want your template to be and how many patterns you want to support you can compose the patterns in the element with individual elements having their own customizations and styles.<pattern-header>\n' +
    '    <pattern-logo small></pattern-logo>\n' +
    '    <pattern-menu size="6"></pattern-menu>\n' +
    '    <pattern-search></pattern-search>\n' +
    '</pattern-header>It’ll all depend on your comfort level with the technology. Remember that the templates inside the elements use the HTML atoms we are already comfortable with so further breaking it down is a matter of personal preference. We’ll go with the more detailed model in the example repositorySample RepositoryThe sample repository https://github.com/caraya/pattern-library is a work in progress for a pattern library that plays with some elements discussed here and I’m using it as a laboratory to experiment with different ideas and concepts.Ideas in issues, comments and PRs area always welcome :-)Links and ResourcesGeneral ResourcesGetting Started With Pattern Libraries — A List ApartPattern Sharing — ClearleftPattern Primer — Jeremy KeithFront end style guides — 24 waysPattern Libraries: What They Are and Why You Need OneResponsive Deliverables — Dave RupertModularity — Tim Berners-LeeAtomic Web DesignAtomic Web DesignAtomic Design MethodologyWeb ComponentsWeb Components: A Tectonic Shift for Web DevelopmentCustom Elements v1: Reusable Web ComponentsShadow DOM v1: Self-Contained Web ComponentsHTML Imports: #include for the webHTML Templates SpecificationHTML’s New Template Tag: standardizing client-side templatingStyling local DOM in PolymerUsing CSS VariablesPattern LibrariesA List ApartMailchimpCode for AmericaUSPTOYahooIBM Design LanguageMicrosoft',
  readingTime: { text: '5 min read', minutes: 4.61, time: 276600, words: 922 }
}
